    </track></source></video></div>
</div>

<title/>
<title>2.14.3: Self Check </title>
<newline/><para><emphasis><emphasis>Following the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<div>
  <para><emphasis>QUESTION:</emphasis> A music store is shipping guitars and snare drums. </para>
  <list>
    <list>
      <item>Each guitar weighs 6 pounds.</item>
      <item>Each snare drum weighs 14 pounds. </item>
      <item>The maximum weight of guitars and snare drums cannot be more than 120 pounds.</item>
      <item>The store needs to ship more than 10 total instruments.</item>
    </list>
  </list>
  <para>Which of the following systems of inequalities describes the situation if \(x\) is the number of guitars and \(y\) is the number of snare drums?</para>
  <table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
          Answers
        </entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
          Feedback
        </entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para>\(6x\;+\;14y\;\geq120\\x+y&gt;10\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The weight of the instruments must be less than 120. The answer is </para>
          <para>\(6x\;+\;14y\;\leq\;120\\x+y\;&gt;\;10\\\).</para>
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para>\(14x+6y\leq120\\x+y\;&gt;10\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The number of guitars is represented by \(x\), and the number of snare drums is represented by \(y\). The answer is </para>
          <para>\(6x\;+\;14y\;\leq\;120\\x+y\;&gt;\;10\\\).</para>
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para>\(14x+6y\geq120\\x+y&lt;10\\\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The weight of the instruments must be less than 120. The number of instruments shipped must be more than 10. The answer is </para>
          <para>\(6x\;+\;14y\;\leq\;120\\x+y\;&gt;\;10\\\).</para>
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry>
          <para>\(6x\;+\;14y\leq\;120\\x+y\;&gt;10\\\)</para>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The weight of the guitars at 6 pounds each and the weight of snare drums at 14 pounds each must be less than or equal to 120 pounds. The number of instruments shipped must be more than 10. This is the correct system of inequalities.</para>
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<title>2.14.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Write a System of Inequalities for a Situation</title>
<para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario to write, graph, and find solutions to a system of inequalities.</para>
<blockquote>Christy sells her photographs at a booth at a street fair. At the start of the day, she wants to have at least 25 photos to display at her booth. Each small photo she displays costs her $4, and each large photo costs her $10. She doesn’t want to spend more than $200 on photos to display.</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write a system of inequalities to model this situation.</item>
</list>
<div>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Identify the Variables.</para>
  <para>Let \(x=\) the number of small photos.</para>
  <para>Let \(y=\) the number of large photos.</para>
  <para><emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-73c8cb94-7fff-3533-ae0a-c632526c0ebc">Step 2 - </emphasis>To find the system of equations, translate the information.
    She wants to have at least 25 photos. The number of small plus the number of large should be at least 25.</para>
  <para>\(x\;+\;y\;\geq25\)</para>
  <para>$4 for each small and $10 for each large must be no more than $200.</para>
  <para>\(4x\;+\;10y\;\leq\;200\)</para>
  <para>To find the system of equations, translate the information.</para>
  <para>She wants to have at least 25 photos.</para>
  <para>The number of small plus the number of large should be at least 25.</para>
  <para>\(x+y\geq25\)</para>
  <para>$4 for each small and $10 for each large must be no more than $200.</para>
  <para>\(4x+10y\leq200\)</para>
  <para><emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-e561840d-7fff-ba79-a3e9-280cef3dc957">Step 3 </emphasis>- Identify constraints based on the context.</para>
  <para>The number of small photos must be greater than or equal to 0.</para>
  <para>\(x\;\geq\;0\)</para>
  <para>The number of large photos must be greater than or equal to 0.</para>
  <para>\(y\;\geq\;0\)</para>
  <emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-2792b861-7fff-13ce-db57-10e78c2d1e72">Step 4</emphasis> - Write the system of inequalities.
  <para>We have our system of equations.</para>
  <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x+y\geq25\\4x+10y\leq200\\x\geq0\\y\geq0\end{array}\right.\)</para>
</div>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2&gt;" type="enumerated">
  <item>Graph the system</item>
</list>
<div>
  <para>Since \(x\geq0\) and \(y\geq0\) (both are greater than or equal to zero), all solutions will be in the first quadrant. As a result, our graph shows only quadrant one.</para>
</div>
<div class="os-raise-align-items-center">
  <div>
    <para>To graph \(x+y\geq25\), graph \(x+y=25\) as a solid line. Choose \((0, 0)\) as a test point. Since it does not make the inequality true, shade (red) the side that does not include the point \((0, 0)\).</para>
    <para>To graph \(4x+10y\leq200\), graph \(4x+10y=200\) as a solid line. Choose \((0, 0)\) as a test point. Since it does make the inequality true, shade (blue) the side that includes the point \((0, 0)\).</para>
  </div>
  <para><figure><media alt="."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5994c54279e0b2909de01c0b8eb4c834d816a76f"/></media></figure></para>
</div>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Could she display 10 small and 20 large photos?</item>
</list>
<para>The solution of the system is the region of the graph that is shaded the darkest. The boundary line sections that border the darkly shaded section are included in the solution, as are the points on the \(x\)-axis from \((25, 0)\) to \((50, 0)\).</para>
<para>To determine if 10 small and 20 large photos would work, we look at the graph to see if the point \((10, 20)\) is in the solution region. We could also test the point to see if it is a solution of both equations.</para>
<para>It is not, so Christy would not display 10 small and 20 large photos.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Could she display 20 large and 10 small photos?</item>
</list>
<div>
  <para>To determine if 20 small and 10 large photos would work, we look at the graph to see if the point \((20, 10)\) is in the solution region. We could also test the point to see if it is a solution of both equations.</para>
  <para>It is, so Christy could choose to display 20 small and 10 large photos.</para>
  <para>Notice that we could also test the possible solutions by substituting the values into each inequality.</para>
</div>
<title>Try It: Write a System of Inequalities for a Situation</title>
<para>A trailer can carry a maximum weight of 160 pounds and a maximum volume of 15 cubic feet. A microwave oven, \(m\), weighs 30 pounds and has 2 cubic feet of volume, while a printer, \(p\), weighs 20 pounds and has 3 cubic feet of volume.</para>
<para>Write a system of inequalities to model this situation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Here is how to write the system of inequalities:</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}30m+20p\leq160\\2m+3p\leq15\end{array}\right.\)</para></section>
</content>
